    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d721">

                <pb facs="0618" n="606" xml:id="pg606"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.01/622: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-07-30T22:00:00+02:00">July 30, 1942—10
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 30—6:24 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4259. After further discussing with Mr. Eden your 3484 July 25, Mr. Eden
                    confirmed the statement in the last paragraph of your message 3515 July 28, 8
                    p.m., that General Spears leftBeirut for Cairo July 27 with General Catroux and
                    a meeting will be held with others in Cairo at which I understand Minister of
                    State Casey will sit in. He assured me that the British have no other than their
                    publicly declared policy with regard to Syria and the Lebanon. He took up the
                    question of General Spears’ services in this field with the Prime Minister but
                    was unable to convince the Prime Minister that General Spears was not doing a
                    good job. As reported in my telegram number 3975 July 18, 2 p.m. there is a
                    close personal friendship between the Prime Minister and General Spears which
                    will make it very difficult to bring about a change in this area. I hope that
                    the conference in Cairo may lead to some improvement.</p>
                <p>Mr. Eden also told me they have agreed that General de Gaulle go out there with
                    the idea of further clarifying the situation. He leaves here next Tuesday and
                    also plans to visit Brazzaville. He expects to be away from London about a
                    month.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
